================================================================================
LAUNCHER KNOWLEDGE BASE
================================================================================
Files:  Launcher.h / Launcher.cpp
Purpose: Start RVGL suspended, inject the mod DLL, resume the game.
         Intended to be called from a UI when the user clicks a launch button.
================================================================================


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PUBLIC API
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LaunchConfig (input struct)
    rvglExePath   — Absolute path to rvgl.exe
    modDllPath    — Absolute path to randomizer.dll
    extraArgs     — Optional CLI args passed to RVGL (e.g. "-window 1920 1080")

LaunchResult (output struct)
    success       — true if RVGL launched and the DLL was injected successfully
    errorMessage  — human-readable failure reason; empty on success
    processPid    — Windows process ID of the launched RVGL instance; 0 on failure

Launcher::Launch(config) → LaunchResult
    The only function the UI needs to call. All other functions are internal.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTERNAL FUNCTIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BuildCommandLine(exePath, extraArgs) → std::string
────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Builds the command line string that CreateProcessA receives.

The exe path is wrapped in double quotes to handle paths containing spaces
(e.g. "C:\Program Files\RVGL\rvgl.exe"). Without quotes, CreateProcessA would
treat each space-separated word as a separate token and misparse the path.
Extra args are appended after a space if present.

Example output:
    "C:\RVGL\rvgl.exe" -window 1920 1080


InjectDll(hProcess, dllPath) → bool
────────────────────────────────────
Loads a DLL into a foreign process using the standard
VirtualAllocEx + WriteProcessMemory + CreateRemoteThread(LoadLibraryA) technique.

hProcess must have been opened (or created) with at least:
    PROCESS_CREATE_THREAD | PROCESS_VM_OPERATION | PROCESS_VM_WRITE

Returns true if LoadLibraryA succeeded inside the target process.

Step 1 — Allocate memory inside RVGL's address space
    VirtualAllocEx(hProcess, ..., pathLen, MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_READWRITE)

    Your process and RVGL's process have completely separate address spaces.
    A pointer valid in your code means nothing in RVGL. VirtualAllocEx asks
    Windows to carve out a small region inside RVGL's address space and returns
    its address from RVGL's perspective. The region is just large enough to hold
    the DLL path string (including null terminator).

Step 2 — Write the DLL path into that memory
    WriteProcessMemory(hProcess, remotePath, dllPath.c_str(), pathLen, nullptr)

    Copies the DLL path string from your process into the memory allocated in
    Step 1. After this call, RVGL's address space contains the string
    "C:\MyMod\randomizer.dll" at address remotePath.

Step 3 — Get the address of LoadLibraryA
    GetModuleHandleA("kernel32.dll") → GetProcAddress(..., "LoadLibraryA")

    LoadLibraryA is the standard Windows function that loads a DLL given a path
    string. It lives in kernel32.dll. The key fact exploited here: kernel32.dll
    is loaded at the same virtual address in every process on the same OS session.
    This means the address looked up in your process is valid to use in RVGL's
    process directly.

Step 4 — Create a thread inside RVGL that calls LoadLibraryA(dllPath)
    CreateRemoteThread(hProcess, ..., loadLibraryA, remotePath, ...)
    WaitForSingleObject(hRemoteThread, INFINITE)

    CreateRemoteThread creates a new thread inside RVGL's process. That thread
    starts executing at loadLibraryA with remotePath as its argument — in other
    words, RVGL ends up calling LoadLibraryA("C:\MyMod\randomizer.dll") from
    inside itself.

    Windows loads the DLL and calls DllMain(DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH), which is where
    MinHook installs the function hooks.

    WaitForSingleObject blocks until the remote thread finishes. This guarantees
    DllMain has completed and all hooks are installed before we proceed to resume
    RVGL's main thread.

    The remote thread's exit code is the return value of LoadLibraryA:
        non-zero (HMODULE) = success
        zero               = failure
    This is how InjectDll knows whether loading succeeded.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Launcher::Launch(config) — STEP BY STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Validate inputs
    Checks that rvglExePath and modDllPath are non-empty before touching any OS
    resources. Fails fast with a clear message rather than producing a cryptic
    Windows error code downstream.

Step 2 — Start RVGL suspended
    CreateProcessA(..., CREATE_SUSPENDED, ...)

    CREATE_SUSPENDED is the critical flag. Windows creates the process — allocates
    its address space, loads rvgl.exe and all its dependent DLLs (SDL2.dll,
    OpenAL32.dll, libGLESv2.dll, etc.) — but the main thread is frozen at the
    very first instruction. No game code has run. RVGL has no idea it is paused.

Step 3 — Inject while the main thread is frozen
    InjectDll(pi.hProcess, config.modDllPath)

    Because RVGL's main thread is suspended, there is no race condition.
    LoadAllCars has not been called, the car pool has not been built, nothing
    has happened. The remote thread created for injection runs independently of
    the main thread and completes fully before the main thread is touched.

Step 4 — Handle injection failure cleanly
    If InjectDll returns false:
        TerminateProcess(pi.hProcess, 1)
        CloseHandle(pi.hThread / pi.hProcess)
        return { false, errorMessage, 0 }

    A failed injection leaves RVGL suspended in memory — invisible, unreachable,
    uncloseable by the user. TerminateProcess kills it immediately. The handles
    are then closed so Windows can fully clean up the process resources.

Step 5 — Resume RVGL
    ResumeThread(pi.hThread)

    Unpauses RVGL's main thread. From RVGL's perspective it simply starts
    normally. It has no knowledge of the injection. The hooks are already patched
    into its code, so when it reaches LoadAllCars or SetupAllRaceCars the mod
    functions run instead of the originals.

Step 6 — Close handles and return
    CloseHandle(pi.hThread)
    CloseHandle(pi.hProcess)
    return { true, "", pid }

    Closing the handles does not kill RVGL. It means the launcher no longer holds
    a reference to the process. RVGL continues running as a fully independent
    process. The PID is returned in LaunchResult in case the UI wants to monitor
    whether the game is still running (e.g. to show a "Game is running" indicator
    or re-enable the launch button when the game closes).


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY CREATE_SUSPENDED ELIMINATES THE RACE CONDITION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Without CREATE_SUSPENDED (the polling injector approach):
    1. Launcher starts RVGL normally
    2. Launcher polls every ~1 second looking for rvgl.exe
    3. RVGL is already running — LoadAllCars may have already been called
    4. Injecting now is too late; the car pool snapshot is missed

With CREATE_SUSPENDED:
    1. RVGL process exists but its main thread has not executed a single instruction
    2. Injection happens and hooks are installed
    3. RVGL is resumed — hooks are guaranteed to be in place before any game code runs

The suspended approach also means the launcher can exit immediately after
ResumeThread. There is no need for it to stay alive.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

cmdLine.data() vs cmdLine.c_str()
    CreateProcessA is documented to potentially write back into the command line
    buffer, which the const pointer returned by c_str() does not permit since
    C++11. data() on a non-const std::string returns a writable pointer.

kernel32.dll address identity
    The guarantee that kernel32.dll loads at the same address in every process
    holds for the lifetime of an OS session but can change after a reboot (ASLR).
    Since we look up the address at runtime via GetProcAddress rather than
    hardcoding it, this is handled correctly automatically.

Permissions
    CreateProcessA with CREATE_SUSPENDED requires no special privileges when
    launching a process your own user account owns. The VM operations
    (VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory, CreateRemoteThread) on a process you
    created also require no elevation. Antivirus software may flag these calls;
    this is a known limitation of the injection approach.
